# =============================================================================
# BitWarden CLI — environment variables
# =============================================================================
# Used by scripts/bw-unlock.sh and the bw CLI skill.
#
# Install: brew install bitwarden-cli  (macOS)
#          sudo snap install bw        (Linux)
#          npm install -g @bitwarden/cli
#
# --- CREDENTIAL STANDARD: BW Master Pass comes from Apple Keychain ---
# The master password is retrieved from Apple Keychain (service:
# Bitwarden-Master) on every unlock by scripts/bw-unlock.sh — it is NEVER
# stored in this file. The API-key halves (Bitworden-ClientID /
# Bitworder-ClientSecret) live in Keychain too. One-time setup:
#   security add-generic-password -s "Bitwarden-Master" -w "your-master-password" login.keychain-db
#   security add-generic-password -s "Bitworder-ClientID" -w "your-client-id" login.keychain-db
#   security add-generic-password -s "Bitworder-ClientSecret" -w "your-client-secret" login.keychain-db
# See references/keychain.md
#
# Login (first time only): bw login  (interactive), or
#   export BW_CLIENTID=... BW_CLIENTSECRET=... && bw login --apikey
# Create an API key at https://vault.bitwarden.com/#/settings/keys
#
# BW_PASSWORD / BW_CLIENTID / BW_CLIENTSECRET below are FALLBACKS ONLY — for
# non-macOS (Linux/Windows, no Keychain) or deliberate overrides. Do NOT set
# BW_PASSWORD on macOS; Keychain is the standard.
# BW_CLIENTID=your-client-id
# BW_CLIENTSECRET=your-client-secret
# BW_PASSWORD=your-master-password
#
# Session key (set automatically after unlock, persists for terminal session):
# BW_SESSION=
